Since $\angle AEB + \angle DE$. Further, since $\angle AEB = \angle ADC$, \[\angle ADC + \angle DBE + \angle DAE + \angle BCE = 180^\circ\]

$\angle ADC = 70^\circ$. So, \[ 70^\circ + \angle DAE + 2y + \angle DBE + 2x = 180^\circ \]since isosceles triangles' base angles are equal, we've made $\angle DAE$ and $\angle DEB$ equal to $2x$.  so, \[ 70^\circ + \angle DAE + 2y + \angle DEA + 2x + \angle BCE =180^\circ \]since isosceles triangles' base angles are equal, we've made $\angle DEB$ and $\angle BCE$ equal to $2y$.  so, \[ 70^\circ + \angle DAE + 2y + \angle DEA + 2x + \angle BCE = 180^\circ \]This matches the internal angle sum of the triangle ($\angle AEC$ is also expanded) or Givens in the diagram. Now, \[ [\angle DCB + \angle DBE] + [\angle DAE + 2x] + \angle BCE = 180^\circ\]

